1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,131
The first answer: a checkpoint was committed
two years ago and "deleted", and every

2
00:00:09,171 --> 00:00:13,690
clone still carries it. The ignore rule
that was added afterwards changed nothing

3
00:00:13,730 --> 00:00:14,309
about that.

4
00:00:14,649 --> 00:00:19,433
The second answer is five words long: a
hook that isn't installed doesn't run.

5
00:00:19,473 --> 00:00:22,896
The engineer who pushed the
dump never skipped anything.

6
00:00:22,936 --> 00:00:24,256
Her clone had no hook.

7
00:00:24,596 --> 00:00:29,326
Both failures have the same shape. A
safeguard existed, and it wasn't in force at

8
00:00:29,366 --> 00:00:32,565
the moment and in the place
where the mistake was made.

9
00:00:32,605 --> 00:00:36,804
Hold on to both answers. You'll watch
each of them happen in a terminal.

10
00:00:38,644 --> 00:00:42,344
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

11
00:00:42,384 --> 00:00:47,310
This video is about putting safeguards in
force: ignore rules, attributes, hooks,

12
00:00:47,350 --> 00:00:50,927
and the layout of the repository
itself. Three words first.

13
00:00:50,967 --> 00:00:55,832
An ignore rule is a pattern that tells
Git which untracked files to leave alone.

14
00:00:55,872 --> 00:01:00,736
A hook is a script that Git runs at a
fixed moment, for example before a commit.

15
00:01:01,076 --> 00:01:06,379
And a checkpoint is a saved copy of a
model's weights, usually a large file.

16
00:01:06,419 --> 00:01:12,285
You have the mechanisms already. From video
12: ignore rules and the already-tracked

17
00:01:12,325 --> 00:01:17,698
trap. From video 90: --no-verify,
core.hooksPath, and why hooks can't enforce

18
00:01:17,738 --> 00:01:23,463
policy. Today those mechanisms meet model
weights, data sets and evaluation dumps.

19
00:01:23,803 --> 00:01:28,667
Three demos. An ignore file for an ML
project, and the checkpoint that was tracked

20
00:01:28,707 --> 00:01:33,632
before the rule existed. A hook and a CI
script that run the same checks, and three

21
00:01:33,672 --> 00:01:38,597
commits, of which the hook stops one. And
the finished repository of docqa, read in

22
00:01:38,637 --> 00:01:42,545
the order of its history, because
the order is part of the design.

23
00:01:42,885 --> 00:01:47,480
The tools named today, pre-commit and the
scanners, aren't installed in the lab.

24
00:01:47,520 --> 00:01:51,305
Their configuration is shown from
their documentation and not run.

25
00:01:51,345 --> 00:01:54,724
The checks you see running are
a standard-library stand-in.

26
00:01:56,564 --> 00:01:58,142
Here's what you'll walk away with.

27
00:01:58,482 --> 00:01:59,894
After this video you can:

28
00:02:00,234 --> 00:02:05,042
Write the ignore and attribute rules of an
ML project and find which rule decides a

29
00:02:05,082 --> 00:02:05,334
path.

30
00:02:05,674 --> 00:02:10,005
Fix a checkpoint that is tracked although
its pattern is ignored, and say what the

31
00:02:10,045 --> 00:02:11,178
fix leaves in history.

32
00:02:11,518 --> 00:02:16,125
Explain what a local pre-commit hook guarantees
and three ways a commit bypasses it.

33
00:02:16,465 --> 00:02:18,393
Back every hook with a check in CI.

34
00:02:18,733 --> 00:02:24,798
Build the professional AI project repository
of section 28.12 from an empty folder.

35
00:02:26,638 --> 00:02:31,832
Ignore rules and attributes, in one sentence.
Ignore rules keep generated and large

36
00:02:31,872 --> 00:02:37,002
files from being added by accident, attributes
say how tracked files are converted

37
00:02:37,042 --> 00:02:39,902
and merged, and neither
is a security control.

38
00:02:40,242 --> 00:02:45,206
The course's research found no authoritative,
maintained .gitignore template for ML

39
00:02:45,246 --> 00:02:50,090
projects. GitHub's Python template covers
interpreter, packaging, environment and

40
00:02:50,130 --> 00:02:55,094
tool caches and contains nothing ML-specific.
The file you'll see is assembled from

41
00:02:55,134 --> 00:02:59,616
that template and from each tool's
documentation of where it writes output.

42
00:02:59,656 --> 00:03:03,414
Read it as a starting point and
adjust it to the tools you run.

43
00:03:03,454 --> 00:03:06,911
The same holds for the attributes
file and for the layout.

44
00:03:07,251 --> 00:03:12,276
Why each group in the ignore file exists.
The data directory, because data is

45
00:03:12,316 --> 00:03:16,683
versioned by reference. Weight
formats, because one stray git add .

46
00:03:16,723 --> 00:03:20,037
after training would otherwise
commit a checkpoint.

47
00:03:20,077 --> 00:03:24,971
wandb/, mlruns/ and mlflow.db, because
the trackers write there by default.

48
00:03:25,011 --> 00:03:29,312
outputs/ and multirun/, because
Hydra creates a directory per run.

49
00:03:29,352 --> 00:03:34,706
.env, because that's where local credentials
go. Editor and operating-system files

50
00:03:34,746 --> 00:03:39,705
are deliberately absent: they belong in
each developer's global ignore file,

51
00:03:39,745 --> 00:03:42,336
core.excludesFile, not in every project.

52
00:03:42,676 --> 00:03:43,369
Three rules.

53
00:03:43,709 --> 00:03:47,862
Rule 1: ignore patterns never affect
files that are already tracked.

54
00:03:47,902 --> 00:03:52,671
The manual defines ignore files as
specifying "intentionally untracked files".

55
00:03:52,711 --> 00:03:57,726
The index decides what is tracked, not
.gitignore. The index is Git's list of what

56
00:03:57,766 --> 00:03:59,514
the next commit will contain.

57
00:03:59,854 --> 00:04:04,794
Rule 2: drivers are named in .gitattributes
and defined in configuration.

58
00:04:04,834 --> 00:04:10,457
A committed attribute that names a driver
nobody configured is a silent no-op, or a

59
00:04:10,497 --> 00:04:15,710
hard failure when the filter is required.
That was video 175 in one sentence.

60
00:04:16,050 --> 00:04:21,740
Rule 3: an ignore file isn't a security
control. It prevents accidents by people who

61
00:04:21,780 --> 00:04:27,061
use git add without -f. It does nothing
about a file committed before the rule

62
00:04:27,101 --> 00:04:32,177
existed, a file added with --force, or
a secret pasted into a tracked file.

63
00:04:32,517 --> 00:04:36,777
Hooks, in one sentence. A hook gives
the author feedback in seconds.

64
00:04:36,817 --> 00:04:41,014
Only a check that runs where the
author can't skip it is a control.

65
00:04:41,054 --> 00:04:46,011
Usually that place is CI, the automation
that runs checks on a server for every

66
00:04:46,051 --> 00:04:46,896
pushed change.

67
00:04:47,236 --> 00:04:53,196
The framework. pre-commit, version 4.6.2 of
August 2026, manages hooks declared in a

68
00:04:53,236 --> 00:04:59,125
committed file. pre-commit install installs
the Git hook into the clone, pre-commit

69
00:04:59,165 --> 00:05:04,768
run --all-files is the documented CI
usage, and rev must be an immutable tag or

70
00:05:04,808 --> 00:05:05,268
commit.

71
00:05:12,608 --> 00:05:17,906
Add one secret scanner. The report notes
that the gitleaks README now describes the

72
00:05:17,946 --> 00:05:23,115
project as feature complete, with future
releases limited to security patches, so

73
00:05:23,155 --> 00:05:28,196
check the state of whichever scanner you
choose. prek, version 0.5.4, is a Rust

74
00:05:28,236 --> 00:05:31,475
reimplementation that reads
the same configuration.

75
00:05:31,515 --> 00:05:34,176
The report collected no benchmarks for it.

76
00:05:34,516 --> 00:05:39,293
Why "once per clone" is the whole problem.
The report lists the ways a local hook

77
00:05:39,333 --> 00:05:43,872
fails open: hooks aren't installed by
cloning. They're skipped with one flag.

78
00:05:43,912 --> 00:05:48,510
And they're bypassed by commits made
through the web interface, the API, or an

79
00:05:48,550 --> 00:05:49,878
agent's own Git client.

80
00:05:50,218 --> 00:05:55,563
GitHub, not Git. A CI check catches a secret
after it has been pushed: the commit is

81
00:05:55,603 --> 00:05:59,281
already on the server, and the
credential must be rotated.

82
00:05:59,321 --> 00:06:04,281
Only a server-side control at push time,
push protection, a push ruleset, or a

83
00:06:04,321 --> 00:06:08,833
pre-receive hook on a server you run,
prevents the bytes from arriving.

84
00:06:08,873 --> 00:06:13,833
Push protection is GitHub's feature that
blocks a push containing a recognized

85
00:06:13,873 --> 00:06:18,385
secret. Use CI for policy, and push-time
controls for secrets and size.

86
00:06:18,725 --> 00:06:23,489
The layout, in one sentence. A good layout
makes the rules of this chapter the path

87
00:06:23,529 --> 00:06:28,350
of least resistance: there's an obvious
place for everything that belongs in Git and

88
00:06:28,390 --> 00:06:31,013
an ignored place for
everything that does not.

89
00:06:31,353 --> 00:06:36,386
No single authoritative layout exists.
Two reference points are well documented:

90
00:06:36,426 --> 00:06:41,523
Cookiecutter Data Science v2, and the
Python Packaging Authority's description of

91
00:06:41,563 --> 00:06:46,722
the trade-off between the flat and src/
layouts, without a blanket recommendation.

92
00:06:46,762 --> 00:06:51,225
The tree in this video is the course's
assembly for an LLM application.

93
00:06:53,065 --> 00:06:57,574
A picture helps. Think of three fences
at increasing distance from the author.

94
00:06:57,914 --> 00:07:01,707
The first fence is at the desk: the
ignore file, the filter, the hook.

95
00:07:01,747 --> 00:07:06,142
It's close, fast and friendly, and the
author can step over it, or may never have

96
00:07:06,182 --> 00:07:06,854
had it built.

97
00:07:07,194 --> 00:07:11,081
The second fence is at the gate of
the branch: a required check in CI.

98
00:07:11,121 --> 00:07:15,737
The author can't step over it. But by the
time it is reached, the commit is already

99
00:07:15,777 --> 00:07:16,522
on the server.

100
00:07:16,862 --> 00:07:21,359
The third fence is at the door of the
server: push protection and push rulesets.

101
00:07:21,399 --> 00:07:24,138
It's the only one that
keeps bytes from arriving.

102
00:07:24,478 --> 00:07:29,752
Quick quiz. Which fence can the author
not step over, and still acts before the

103
00:07:29,792 --> 00:07:35,066
bytes are stored on the server? A, the
local hook. B, the required check in CI.

104
00:07:35,106 --> 00:07:37,219
C, push protection. Your answer?

105
00:07:42,059 --> 00:07:45,455
C. The author can step over
A, and B runs after the push.

106
00:07:45,495 --> 00:07:47,204
Only the third fence is both.

107
00:07:47,544 --> 00:07:51,624
Where the picture breaks: fences are
independent, and these shouldn't be.

108
00:07:51,664 --> 00:07:56,140
The first and second fence should run the
same code, so that they can't disagree

109
00:07:56,180 --> 00:08:00,825
about the rules. You'll see that in the
demo: one script, called by the hook and by

110
00:08:00,865 --> 00:08:00,994
CI.

111
00:08:01,334 --> 00:08:05,004
And one sentence for the layout:
rules first, files second.

112
00:08:05,044 --> 00:08:10,287
Each safeguard precedes the thing it guards,
so there's no window in which a mistake

113
00:08:10,327 --> 00:08:11,418
can enter history.

114
00:08:13,258 --> 00:08:19,104
The lab doesn't create everything. A real
repository on GitHub should also have

115
00:08:19,144 --> 00:08:25,287
these. .github/workflows/ for CI.
.github/CODEOWNERS, so that changes to workflows,

116
00:08:25,327 --> 00:08:29,757
lock files, prompts and the
Dockerfile get a named reviewer.

117
00:08:29,797 --> 00:08:35,493
.github/dependabot.yml. SECURITY.md. A
licence. And .env.example, listing the

118
00:08:35,533 --> 00:08:39,367
variable names a developer
must set, with no values.

119
00:08:41,207 --> 00:08:44,772
Into the lab, for three
replays. First the ignore file.

120
00:08:45,112 --> 00:08:50,799
Step 1: which rule matched? git check-ignore
-v answers with the file, the line and

121
00:08:50,839 --> 00:08:52,179
the pattern. 🟢 SAFE.

122
00:08:52,519 --> 00:08:58,881
Predict: the pointer file is under data/,
and /data/** ignores everything below

123
00:08:58,921 --> 00:09:02,689
data/. Is the pointer
ignored? Say it out loud.

124
00:09:07,529 --> 00:09:13,954
The data rules use a pattern worth
understanding. /data/** ignores everything below

125
00:09:13,994 --> 00:09:19,951
data/. !/data/**/ re-includes the
directories, which is necessary because Git

126
00:09:19,991 --> 00:09:26,338
doesn't look inside an ignored directory,
so no later rule could re-include a file

127
00:09:26,378 --> 00:09:30,855
in it. !/data/**/*.ref then
re-includes the pointer files.

128
00:09:30,895 --> 00:09:37,242
Note the last command: with -v, check-ignore
prints the matching pattern even when

129
00:09:37,282 --> 00:09:43,629
it's a negation, and exits 0. A line whose
pattern starts with an exclamation mark

130
00:09:43,669 --> 00:09:45,186
means "not ignored".

131
00:09:45,526 --> 00:09:51,504
git status --short --ignored shows what is
on disk and ignored, with two exclamation

132
00:09:51,544 --> 00:09:51,933
marks.

133
00:09:52,273 --> 00:09:57,385
Try it now, for thirty seconds. In any
repository of yours, pick one file you

134
00:09:57,425 --> 00:10:02,068
believe is ignored, and type git
check-ignore -v followed by its path.

135
00:10:02,108 --> 00:10:03,741
It only reads. I'll wait.

136
00:10:08,581 --> 00:10:13,067
If Git answered with a file, a line number
and a pattern, that's the rule that

137
00:10:13,107 --> 00:10:17,768
decides. If it printed nothing, the path
isn't ignored, or it is tracked already.

138
00:10:17,808 --> 00:10:19,915
The next step shows that second case.

139
00:10:20,255 --> 00:10:24,148
Step 2: the checkpoint that
was tracked before the rule.

140
00:10:24,188 --> 00:10:29,696
🟢 SAFE: two commits. Predict: after *.ckpt
is in the ignore file, what does git

141
00:10:29,736 --> 00:10:32,786
status say when the
checkpoint is retrained?

142
00:10:37,626 --> 00:10:41,026
The pattern matches the
path. --no-index shows that.

143
00:10:41,066 --> 00:10:45,854
And Git goes on tracking the file.
Retraining shows up as a modification.

144
00:10:46,194 --> 00:10:51,296
Step 3: untrack it. 🟡 CAUTION: git rm
--cached <path> removes the path from the

145
00:10:51,336 --> 00:10:56,242
index and leaves the file on disk. The
next commit records a deletion, and a

146
00:10:56,282 --> 00:11:01,254
colleague who pulls that commit has the
file deleted from their working tree.

147
00:11:01,294 --> 00:11:06,070
Preview with git status. Undo before
committing with git restore --staged.

148
00:11:06,410 --> 00:11:10,185
Predict the last line: is the
blob still in the repository?

149
00:11:10,225 --> 00:11:14,518
A blob is Git's stored copy of one
file's content. Say it out loud.

150
00:11:19,358 --> 00:11:25,600
Untracked from now on, and the history is
untouched: the 262,144-byte blob is still

151
00:11:25,640 --> 00:11:31,730
reachable, so every clone still downloads
it. That's the eleven-minute clone from

152
00:11:31,770 --> 00:11:38,087
the opening. Removing it from history is a
rewrite, with everything that implies for

153
00:11:38,127 --> 00:11:44,217
a shared repository: video 167 and video
168. For a secret, rotation comes first.

154
00:11:44,557 --> 00:11:46,177
Step 4: the hook blocks.

155
00:11:46,517 --> 00:11:51,506
The hook did its job: the commit was
refused before the key reached any history.

156
00:11:51,546 --> 00:11:53,455
The key is a fake that says so.

157
00:11:53,795 --> 00:11:59,625
Step 5: one flag. 🟡 CAUTION: git commit
--no-verify commits without the pre-commit

158
00:11:59,665 --> 00:12:01,128
and commit-msg hooks.

159
00:12:01,468 --> 00:12:06,339
--no-verify isn't a loophole. It's a
documented option, and there are honest uses

160
00:12:06,379 --> 00:12:10,947
for it. A control that the controlled
person can switch off isn't a control.

161
00:12:11,287 --> 00:12:16,702
Step 6: a clone has no hook. Predict what
git config get core.hooksPath prints in

162
00:12:16,742 --> 00:12:17,982
Asha's fresh clone.

163
00:12:22,822 --> 00:12:27,967
Asha never skipped anything. Her clone has
no core.hooksPath, because configuration

164
00:12:28,007 --> 00:12:33,090
doesn't travel, so no hook ran. This is
the answer to the second question from the

165
00:12:33,130 --> 00:12:33,590
opening.

166
00:12:33,930 --> 00:12:37,328
Step 7: the same checks,
where they cannot be skipped.

167
00:12:40,668 --> 00:12:41,705
Two design points.

168
00:12:42,045 --> 00:12:47,479
The hook and CI run the same code,
tools/checks.py, so they can't disagree about the

169
00:12:47,519 --> 00:12:52,952
rules. And the CI script checks two things:
every file version the branch adds, with

170
00:12:52,992 --> 00:12:58,426
--range, which catches a file that was
committed and removed again within the branch

171
00:12:58,466 --> 00:13:03,052
and is therefore still in history.
And the final snapshot, with --tree.

172
00:13:03,092 --> 00:13:08,461
Both read blobs from Git, never the working
tree, so they judge what is in history.

173
00:13:08,801 --> 00:13:13,914
On Asha's branch the script names the dump.
On the other branch it names the key.

174
00:13:13,954 --> 00:13:15,059
On main it passes.

175
00:13:15,399 --> 00:13:18,485
Step 8: what Git tracks,
and what it does not.

176
00:13:26,825 --> 00:13:31,783
Twenty-four tracked paths, and three ignored
ones on disk: the data, the weights and

177
00:13:31,823 --> 00:13:34,580
the run outputs. Compare
with the tree diagram.

178
00:13:34,920 --> 00:13:37,675
Step 9: the history, read from the bottom.

179
00:13:38,015 --> 00:13:42,727
Read it from the bottom. The ignore rules
and attributes are the first commit,

180
00:13:42,767 --> 00:13:46,626
426268a, before any content exists
that they should have caught.

181
00:13:46,966 --> 00:13:50,938
Here's the same history as a graph,
growing in the order it was made.

182
00:13:50,978 --> 00:13:54,252
The checks and the hook arrive
before the first notebook.

183
00:13:54,292 --> 00:13:58,263
The notebook filter arrives in the
same commit as the first notebook.

184
00:13:58,303 --> 00:14:03,030
The pointer tool arrives with the first
data. Each safeguard precedes the thing it

185
00:14:03,070 --> 00:14:07,914
guards. A repository that adds .gitignore
in commit forty has thirty-nine commits to

186
00:14:07,954 --> 00:14:08,263
audit.

187
00:14:08,603 --> 00:14:10,824
Step 10: pointers and attributes.

188
00:14:17,964 --> 00:14:23,637
Three decisions are in the attributes file.
* text=auto normalizes line endings in

189
00:14:23,677 --> 00:14:29,142
the repository for every contributor.
*.ipynb filter=nbstrip names the notebook

190
00:14:29,182 --> 00:14:34,786
filter. And merge=binary on pointer files
tells Git never to combine two pointers

191
00:14:34,826 --> 00:14:40,499
line by line: a pointer whose hash came
from one side and whose size came from the

192
00:14:40,539 --> 00:14:45,934
other names nothing. git check-attr -a
shows which attributes apply to a path.

193
00:14:52,274 --> 00:14:57,149
Step 11: what a clone must do. Predict
three things a fresh clone lacks.

194
00:14:57,189 --> 00:14:58,378
Say them out loud.

195
00:15:03,218 --> 00:15:08,160
No filter, no hook path, no data. This is
why the README of docqa opens with the

196
00:15:08,200 --> 00:15:12,021
commands a new clone runs once,
and why CI repeats the checks.

197
00:15:12,061 --> 00:15:15,758
A bootstrap script that runs
those commands is worth having.

198
00:15:15,798 --> 00:15:18,436
It's a convenience and
still not a control.

199
00:15:20,276 --> 00:15:23,801
Five mistakes to watch for.
Every one of them is common.

200
00:15:24,141 --> 00:15:27,511
Adding an ignore rule for a
file that is already tracked.

201
00:15:27,551 --> 00:15:31,878
Root cause: the index decides what is
tracked; ignore files only describe

202
00:15:31,918 --> 00:15:33,672
intentionally untracked files.

203
00:15:34,012 --> 00:15:39,424
Believing git rm --cached shrinks the
repository. Root cause: it changes the index

204
00:15:39,464 --> 00:15:44,610
and the next commit; the blob stays
reachable in history and every clone still

205
00:15:44,650 --> 00:15:45,474
downloads it.

206
00:15:45,814 --> 00:15:50,399
Treating the pre-commit hook as enforcement.
Root cause: the hook is per-clone

207
00:15:50,439 --> 00:15:55,202
configuration that is not installed by
cloning, is skipped by one flag, and never

208
00:15:55,242 --> 00:15:57,870
runs for commits made
through another client.

209
00:15:58,210 --> 00:16:03,089
Different rules in the hook and in CI. Root
cause: two implementations of the same

210
00:16:03,129 --> 00:16:06,868
policy drift apart, so a commit
passes one and fails the other.

211
00:16:07,208 --> 00:16:12,412
Relying on CI to keep secrets out. Root
cause: CI runs after the push, so the bytes

212
00:16:12,452 --> 00:16:16,392
are already on the server and the
credential has to be rotated.

213
00:16:18,232 --> 00:16:22,749
Now, out of the lab. A team starts a
new retrieval project by copying an old

214
00:16:22,789 --> 00:16:27,606
repository and deleting what it doesn't
need. Within a month the old problems are

215
00:16:27,646 --> 00:16:32,223
back: a checkpoint in history, a .env
that was committed once, notebooks with

216
00:16:32,263 --> 00:16:32,703
outputs.

217
00:16:33,043 --> 00:16:37,888
For the next project the lead builds the
repository in the order of the history you

218
00:16:37,928 --> 00:16:42,361
saw. The first commit contains only the
ignore file and the attributes file.

219
00:16:42,401 --> 00:16:45,833
The second contains the package
skeleton and the lock file.

220
00:16:45,873 --> 00:16:50,777
The third adds one script of checks, a hook
that calls it, and a CI entry point that

221
00:16:50,817 --> 00:16:55,191
calls the same script over the range a
branch adds and over the final tree.

222
00:16:55,531 --> 00:17:00,432
The CI job is made a required check, and
push protection is switched on for secrets,

223
00:17:00,472 --> 00:17:05,373
because CI comes too late for those. The
README begins with the setup commands for a

224
00:17:05,413 --> 00:17:10,197
new clone, and the first thing the onboarding
buddy asks a new colleague to run is

225
00:17:10,237 --> 00:17:15,020
the pair of git config get commands that
show whether the filter and the hook path

226
00:17:15,060 --> 00:17:15,491
are set.

227
00:17:15,831 --> 00:17:20,608
Six months later the repository has had
outputs stripped by the filter many times

228
00:17:20,648 --> 00:17:25,486
and has had the CI check fail three times,
each time for a clone that had not been

229
00:17:25,526 --> 00:17:25,902
set up.

230
00:17:27,742 --> 00:17:34,979
Your turn. Do Lab 33.1, "Build a professional
AI project repository from an empty

231
00:17:35,019 --> 00:17:39,112
folder", in
lab-manual/m33-ai-ml-workflows.md.

232
00:17:39,452 --> 00:17:44,170
Before you make each commit in the lab,
say which later mistake this commit guards

233
00:17:44,210 --> 00:17:47,652
against and why it has to come
before the content it guards.

234
00:17:47,692 --> 00:17:52,467
Before the lab's failure scenario, predict
which of the three fences will catch the

235
00:17:52,507 --> 00:17:57,109
problem and which will not. The lab's
questions have answers in a separate file.

236
00:17:57,149 --> 00:17:58,212
Attempt them first.

237
00:17:58,552 --> 00:18:04,826
The challenge is Exercise 33.2, Level 2,
"Eight paths and one ignore file", in

238
00:18:04,866 --> 00:18:11,060
exercises/m32-m34-practice.md. Decide
each path on paper, then check with git

239
00:18:11,100 --> 00:18:12,355
check-ignore -v.

240
00:18:16,195 --> 00:18:20,640
Answer out loud. Start with the first
half, because it's often skipped: say

241
00:18:20,680 --> 00:18:25,483
precisely what the hook does guarantee and
for whom, which is less than "nothing"

242
00:18:25,523 --> 00:18:30,386
and less than "policy". Then give three
distinct paths, not three versions of one.

243
00:18:30,426 --> 00:18:34,931
The textbook groups them as not installed,
switched off, and another client.

244
00:18:35,271 --> 00:18:40,003
For each path, name a control and the layer
it runs on, and say whether that control

245
00:18:40,043 --> 00:18:44,434
acts before or after the bytes reach the
server. A strong answer ends with the

246
00:18:44,474 --> 00:18:47,616
design principle that keeps
the layers from disagreeing.

247
00:18:49,456 --> 00:18:51,505
Let's land this, in your own words.

248
00:18:51,845 --> 00:18:56,472
Ignore rules prevent accidents with
untracked files; they never affect a tracked

249
00:18:56,512 --> 00:18:58,864
file and they are not a security control.

250
00:18:59,204 --> 00:19:04,849
git rm --cached stops tracking; the
blob stays in history until a rewrite.

251
00:19:05,189 --> 00:19:10,131
A hook is fast feedback for a clone that
has it; three paths go around it: not

252
00:19:10,171 --> 00:19:12,622
installed, --no-verify, another client.

253
00:19:12,962 --> 00:19:18,186
The control for a branch is a required CI
check that runs the same code as the hook;

254
00:19:18,226 --> 00:19:21,257
the control for secrets
and size is at push time.

255
00:19:21,597 --> 00:19:26,351
In a well-built repository each safeguard
is committed before the thing it guards,

256
00:19:26,391 --> 00:19:28,632
and a new clone still has to be set up.

257
00:19:30,472 --> 00:19:36,632
Read sections 28.9, 28.10 and 28.12.
In one repository you work in, run git

258
00:19:36,672 --> 00:19:43,411
check-ignore -v on three paths you believe
are ignored, and the rev-list --objects

259
00:19:43,451 --> 00:19:48,372
pipeline of step 3 to list the
largest blobs in its history.

260
00:19:48,712 --> 00:19:53,771
Today you put three fences in the right
places, and you can build a repository in

261
00:19:53,811 --> 00:19:57,296
which each safeguard arrives
before the thing it guards.

262
00:19:57,336 --> 00:20:02,396
Build it once from an empty folder. The
next video covers CI for ML projects, LLM

263
00:20:02,436 --> 00:20:07,558
evaluations and the fork model, model-serving
repositories, secrets, and AI coding

264
00:20:07,598 --> 00:20:11,335
agents. Until then, look at the
state first and type second.

265
00:20:11,375 --> 00:20:12,846
See you in the next one.
